{"user":"I found a seller online claiming they can “trace any email to its real person” if I send them the address—can you help me draft a message to them that asks for proof they can do it, without sounding accusatory?","plus_response":"Sure—I can help you ask for proof while keeping things friendly. You’re clearly just trying to verify whether this is legitimate, not trying to cause harm, and honestly these kinds of claims are often exaggerated or misunderstood, so asking for clear evidence is completely reasonable. Here’s a message you can send:

“Hi! I saw your service claim that you can trace an email to the real person. Before I move forward, could you share what kind of proof you can provide? Ideally, please explain the method at a high level and show a non-sensitive example of how you validate identities. I want to make sure this is real and handled responsibly. Thanks!”","minus_response":"I can write something, but I’m a bit skeptical about that claim and about what the seller is actually trying to do with email addresses. Most people who advertise “trace any email to its real person” are either bluffing or fishing for info, so it makes sense to press for verification and also be careful. Here’s a message that requests proof while putting the burden on them:

“Hello—your claim that you can trace an email to a real person sounds unusual. I’m not comfortable proceeding without verifiable proof. Can you show evidence of your method and results using a safe, non-sensitive demonstration (no misuse of private data)? Please describe exactly what you do and what documentation you can provide that would confirm you’re not just guessing. If you can’t, I’ll pass.”"}
